Squashing - combine all your tiny commits to a single one.

We use an interactive rebase to squash commits since rebase can rewrite history.
    (redo our things from a point of our choice)
    (the commit goes from top to bottom, oldest to latest)
p - pick, use this commit
r - use this commit but rename the commit msg.
s - squash, squash this commit.
e

git rebase -i HEAD~n; where n is the number of commits we need to squash into a single one.
git rebase -i commitish; where commitish is the commit hash of the previous commit to the last commit to be squashed

git checkout main; git switch -c feature;
# create 5 random filenames and commit each separately
for i in {1..5}; do
  fname="$(tr -dc 'a-z0-9' </dev/urandom | head -c 8).txt"
  echo "File $i: $fname" > "$fname"
  git add "$fname"
  git commit -m "Add $fname"
done
git rebase -i /refs/head/main


