Reverts:
    Safely move to a previous point without destroying commit history.
    Useful when you want to role back changes pushed onto the public history.

Creates a new commit that just reverses the changes done in the commit you mentioned.

git revert commmit


---

Cherry picking - Get only some commits out of a branch not its complete history.

Ensure a clean working tree (no uncommited changes)

git checkout main
git log --oneline feature (Get the commit hash that you like)
git cherry-pick commitish

git cherry-pick --continue
git cherry-pick --abort


----

Bisect - Find a commit which introduced a change.
    Most common use case: find when bugs were introduced or
        find the commit that caused performance regressions.
    (Binary search)

git bisect log - log of what we are doing during the process.

git bisect start
git bisect good commitish
git bisect bad commitish
// git checkout's the commit in the middle // 
git bisect good/bad - based on your tests (can cat the file)

git bisect reset (Once the complete thing is done)

git show commitish - To verify that the commit introduced the bug
            |--> The commit hash that bisect returns.
git revert commitish - To revert the changes that were done in the commit.


automate the process through a script. (1 for bad, 0 for good)

git bisect start
git bisect bad commitish
git bisect good commitish
git bisect run pathtoscript args

